"The logic follows directly from what this unit has already covered about the twelve meridians running along the inner or outer surface of each limb. A stretch that extends the inner arm from shoulder to fingertip, palm rotated outward, follows the same path this course earlier assigned to the Lung meridian; a stretch reaching down the back of the leg follows a path associated with the Bladder meridian. The claim isn't that stretching a muscle group and \"moving qi along a meridian\" are two different physical events — it's that describing the same movement through the meridian map adds a layer of intention and attention that plain stretching, done without that map in mind, doesn't include.": "434c70c1a808",
"This connects two things this course has kept mostly separate until now: the passive picture of meridians as something a practitioner presses from the outside, and an active picture of a person moving their own body along that same map. Read together, they suggest that meridians were never meant to describe only what a doctor does to a patient — they were also meant to describe what a person could learn to do for themselves.": "58ab91ca5aa4",
"This course has already described meridians as mapped patterns of connection rather than physical pipes, and acupuncture as one way of interacting with that map by pressing a specific point. There's a second, entirely different way of interacting with the same map: movement. A category of practice sometimes called \"guided stretching along the meridians\" designs specific stretches, twists, and postures to trace the exact path of a chosen meridian through the body, rather than stretching muscles in whatever shape feels convenient.": "f4546fce14c6",
